*Proof:* After the environment shift, staleness risk of affected artifacts jumps above \(\theta_{\text{stale}}\) because the drift term \(D_{\text{drift}}\) becomes large. The auditor will therefore schedule probes at an increased rate. Since each probe has a positive probability of generating successful contestation (the shift signal is strong enough), detection occurs with probability 1 by the second Borel‑Cantelli lemma. The expected latency is the inverse of the probe rate times the success probability, which is smaller than the passive observation rate because the auditor actively targets those artifacts. \(\square\)

**Theorem 6.2 (Bounded Instability)**  
*Statement:* During transition, the probability that epistemic EDS leaves \([0.3,0.7]\) decays exponentially with time.

